<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Colaeus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 638 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> · Also known as Kolaios</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Colaeus was a Samian merchant whose voyage is told by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>. Around 638 BCE, bound for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, his ship was driven west by storms through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-gibraltar">Strait of Gibraltar</a> to Tartessos in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, then an untouched market, and he returned with an enormous profit. He dedicated a tenth of it at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraion-of-samos">Heraion of Samos</a> as a great bronze cauldron decorated with griffins' heads.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/colaeus.jpg" alt="Colaeus" width="320" height="486" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hubertl · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kolaios_of_Samos,_figure_at_the_Naturhistorisches_Museum,_Vienna_8940.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
